.Dd November 20, 2026
.Dt LOOP 4
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm loop ,
.Nm loop-control
.Nd devices backed by regular files
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The four loop devices,
.Pa /dev/loop0
through
.Pa /dev/loop3 ,
allow a file system in a regular file to be mounted.
They are block devices owned by root with mode 0660, Linux's loop major
number 7, and minor numbers 0 through 3, and are there whether or not a
file is attached.
A node reads any byte range of its device and writes whole sectors at
offsets of whole sectors, as a disk node does, with the rule of
.Dv O_DIRECT
of
.Xr disk 4 ,
and answers
.Er ENXIO
with nothing attached; a write needs root, a device attached for writing,
and no file system mounted on it, and one at or past the end returns
.Er ENOSPC ,
while one that crosses it writes the sectors before it.
.Pp
The control node,
.Pa /dev/loop-control ,
is owned by root with mode 0600, major number 240, and minor number 36.
Only root can attach a file.
The node accepts one command per write:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ql attach N /path
Attach the file to device
.Ar N .
.It Ql attach - /path
Attach the file to the first free device.
.It Ql attach ro N /path
Attach the file to device
.Ar N
read-only.
Every mount of this device is read-only, regardless of the requested options.
.It Ql detach N
Detach device
.Ar N .
.El
.Pp
Device numbers range from 0 through 3.
.Xr loopctl 8
writes these commands.
.Pp
The nodes also take Linux's loop ioctls, which util-linux's
.Xr losetup 8
and
.Xr mount 8
make:
.Bl -tag -width LOOP_SET_STATUS64
.It Dv LOOP_CTL_GET_FREE
On
.Pa /dev/loop-control :
the number of the first free device, or
.Er ENOSPC .
.Dv LOOP_CTL_ADD
of a device there is
.Er EEXIST ,
and
.Dv LOOP_CTL_REMOVE
.Er EBUSY ,
since the four are made at boot.
.It Dv LOOP_SET_FD , Dv LOOP_CONFIGURE
Attach the file open on the descriptor given, by the path
.Pa /proc/self/fd
gives it, read-only unless both that descriptor and the node's are open
for writing.
A
.Vt struct loop_config
with a block size other than 0 or 512, and a
.Vt struct loop_info64
with an offset, a size limit or an encryption, are
.Er EINVAL .
.It Dv LO_FLAGS_AUTOCLEAR
In the flags of either: detach the device when the last description of
its node is closed and no file system is mounted on it, which is when
.Ql mount -o loop
lets go of it at the unmount.
.It Dv LOOP_GET_STATUS64
The device number, the file's inode number, the first 63 bytes of the
path it was attached by, and the flags
.Dv LO_FLAGS_READ_ONLY
and
.Dv LO_FLAGS_AUTOCLEAR ;
.Er ENXIO
with nothing attached.
.It Dv LOOP_SET_STATUS64
Sets
.Dv LO_FLAGS_AUTOCLEAR
or clears it.
.It Dv LOOP_CLR_FD
Detaches the device, or, while a file system is mounted on it or another
description of its node is open, sets it to clear itself.
.It Dv LOOP_SET_DIRECT_IO , Dv LOOP_SET_BLOCK_SIZE , Dv LOOP_SET_CAPACITY
Take 0, 512 and any value, which change nothing, and
.Er EINVAL
otherwise.
.El
.Pp
An attached device is a disk of
.Pa /sys/block ,
with its backing file in
.Pa /sys/block/loopN/loop/backing_file ,
and of
.Pa /proc/partitions ,
as on Linux.
.Pp
An attached file must be regular and have a positive length that is a
whole number of sectors.
Attachment waits for a concurrent KRFFS relocation before opening
the backing file.
A KRFFS file can start inside a disk sector.
The device reports that offset to a mounted KRFFS volume so that its
metadata writes are ordered by the backing disk's sectors.
A file may have only one attachment; another hard link to it is the
same file for this purpose.
A file on a loop-mounted volume cannot be attached to another loop device.
A chain may contain only one loop device.
.Pp
While attached, the file cannot be written, grown by
.Xr fallocate 2 ,
mapped shared through a descriptor open for writing, or written by a core
dump, through any mount that shows it, a bind included.
Its length may change, by
.Xr truncate 2 ,
.Xr ftruncate 2
or an open with
.Dv O_TRUNC ,
as on Linux: the device keeps the size it was attached with, reads past
the file's new end give zeros, and a write there extends the file.
On KRFFS, the boot root among them, a change of length may move the
file, which would change the offset in a sector the device reported to
the volume on it, so there it is refused.
On the KRFFS boot root it cannot be unlinked or renamed over either; on
every other file system it then loses its name and the device keeps the
file until the detach, as on Linux.
A file on tmpfs is not attached while a shared mapping through a
descriptor open for writing maps it, or once it is sealed against
shrinking, which
.Xr udmabuf 4
and
.Xr vgpu-shm 4
require before they hand its pages on; neither takes a file that is
attached.
The volume containing the file cannot be unmounted until the device
is detached.
These operations fail with
.Er EBUSY .
.Pp
Detach fails with
.Er EBUSY
while the device has a mount, a buffer is held, a transfer is in
progress, or an attachment is still in progress, and with
.Er ENXIO
when another detach released the device while this one wrote its
buffers.
Removing a medium marks the loop devices attached to files on it as gone;
one with a mount or a transfer in progress is released when that ends.
.Pp
.Pa /proc/loop
has one line per occupied device, giving the device, its size in bytes,
whether it accepts writes, its write and sync counts, and the first 63
bytes of the path used to attach it, or
.Ql (gone)
for a retained device whose medium was removed.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width /dev/loop-control
.It Pa /dev/loop0 No through Pa /dev/loop3
Loop devices, block devices 7:0 through 7:3.
.It Pa /dev/loop-control
Attachment and detachment commands.
.It Pa /proc/loop
Attachments and counters.
.El
.Sh DIAGNOSTICS
.Bl -tag -width EACCES
.It Er EACCES
The caller is not root.
.It Er EBUSY
The file is already attached, an operation conflicts with an attachment,
or the device cannot yet be detached.
.It Er EINVAL
The file is not regular, its length is zero or not a whole number of
sectors, the device number is outside 0 through 3, a write of a node is
not of whole sectors, or a transfer of a node opened with
.Dv O_DIRECT
is off the sector.
.It Er ELOOP
The file is on a loop-mounted volume.
.It Er ENXIO
Nothing is attached to the device, or it was detached while a detach
wrote its buffers.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr mount 2 ,
.Xr disk 4 ,
.Xr loopctl 8 ,
.Xr losetup 8 ,
.Xr mount 8
